+\subsection{Selected surface-owned cohesive history}
+The optional \texttt{Cohesive history representation = surface elastic slip}
+uses $Z_h=\sum_i N_i I_{h,i}^{\rm old}q_i^{\rm old}$ as the authoritative
+Q1 internal variable. At a point, $q^{\rm old}=Z_h/I_h^{\rm old}$; the product
+of separately interpolated $I_h$ and $q$ is not used. With uniform $G_\Gamma$
+and temperature-independent uniform viscosity, advance
+\[
+ Z_i^{\rm new}=\beta Z_i^{\rm old}+\kappa V_i,\qquad
+ q_i^{\rm new}=Z_i^{\rm new}/I_{h,i}^{\rm new}.
+\]
+The pointwise trial law, its derivative, and accepted publication are therefore
+identical in this representation. No surface--particle--surface transfer occurs.
+Nonuniform coefficients are rejected for this option; multiplication would
+otherwise leave Q1. The existing q and previous-I property slots retain the
+nodal data, with a representation marker that forbids switching on restart.
+The default \texttt{particle projection} and generic projector are unchanged.
+Changing phase retains the profile correction
+$C=\beta Z_h^{\rm old}(h/I_h-h^{\rm old}/I_h^{\rm old})/\kappa$.
+This repair does not establish bulk/fault work conjugacy or phase-energy
+consistency. See \texttt{propagation/P2\_coupling\_derivation.md}.
+
+\subsection{Selected cohesive work-conjugate pair}
+The separate optional \texttt{Fault mechanical work measure = bulk conjugate}
+requires one straight two-dimensional cohesive fault, the surface elastic-slip
+representation, and prescribed friction pressure. Define
+$(LV)_a=\chi_a\sum_iN_i(s_a)V_i\boldsymbol S_a$ on the actual owned Stokes
+quadrature points, with their actual phase and incoming FE stress. Then
+\[
+ R_i=\sum_a JxW_a\chi_aN_i(s_a)(\boldsymbol\tau_a:\boldsymbol S_a+
+ \tau_{{\rm bg},a})-
+ \sum_b ds_bN_i(s_b)(q_b+\mu_bP_b+\eta^d_bV_b).
+\]
+The unchanged bulk slip source is $L$; its surface shear partner is its
+$JxW$ adjoint, not a particle-volume average. Current and previous phase are
+sampled separately, retaining the common profile correction $C$ in both blocks.
+The corresponding $G$ is the derivative of this shear load, and
+\[
+ K_{ij}=\sum_a JxW_a\,2\kappa_{{\rm bulk},a}\chi_a^2N_iN_j
+ (\boldsymbol S_a:\boldsymbol S_a)
+ +\sum_b ds_bN_iN_j(\kappa_\Gamma/I_h+P\mu_V+\eta^d).
+\]
+Resistance and its derivative use the same three-point surface Gauss rule;
+the residual mass uses $ds$. This avoids weighting cohesive energy by the
+approximately normalized bulk quadrature of $\chi$. The default particle-volume
+path and the separately guarded frozen-mature bulk-work option remain intact.
+The selected work representation is fixed through restart. Discrete adjointness
+does not remove particle/FE stress-transfer errors or endpoint-in-time splitting
+errors in integrated work, and does not qualify the separate phase-energy
+projection approximation.
 
